  "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $\u0394$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u(v+1),\\,4u(v-u)) \\\\  & =(u(1+1),\\,4u(1-u)) \\\\  & =(2u,\\,4u(1-u)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=4u(1-u),\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=4t(1-t),\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny=2x(1-\\frac{x}{2})\\,\u21d2\\,y=-(x-1)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich is a downward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to ((1)(1+1),\\,4(1)(1-1))=(2,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to ((0)(1+1),\\,4(0)(1-0))=(0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,0)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14096545/assets/970003245107516d9be7b4729d6c18abf01d1f48c82211402f06d13cec4e3a08.png)",
